Interactive

Edit the robot’s reasoning
The two networks of Figure 4 (seed 0, 2,000 demos) on 12 held-out scenes. Choose what goes into the reasoning slot: the network’s own reasoning, the other object’s position, or any point you set. Your browser runs both networks.

editData = FileAttachment("data/w10-edit.json").json()
viewof editScene = Inputs.range([1, 12], {step: 1, value: 1, label: "scene"})
viewof editSlot = Inputs.radio(["its own reasoning", "the other object", "a point I choose"],
                               {value: "its own reasoning", label: "reasoning slot"})
viewof editX = Inputs.range([-1, 1], {step: 0.05, value: 0, label: "chosen x"})
viewof editY = Inputs.range([-1, 1], {step: 0.05, value: 0, label: "chosen y"})





































mlp = (layers, x) =&gt; layers.reduce((h, L, k) =&gt; {
  const z = L.b.map((b, j) =&gt; b + h.reduce((s, v, i) =&gt; s + v * L.W[i][j], 0));
  return k === layers.length - 1 ? z : z.map(Math.tanh);
}, x)
editOut = {
  const s = editData.scenes[editScene - 1];
  const res = {};
  for (const m of ["ecot", "dropout"]) {
    const own = mlp(editData.nets[m], [...s.obs, 0, 0, 0]).slice(0, 2);
    const other = s.pos[1 - s.tgt];
    const slot = editSlot === "its own reasoning" ? own : editSlot === "the other object" ? other : [editX, editY];
    const a = mlp(editData.nets[m], [...s.obs, ...slot, 1]).slice(2, 4);
    res[m] = {slot, end: [s.grip[0] + a[0], s.grip[1] + a[1]]};
  }
  return res;
}
















{
  const s = editData.scenes[editScene - 1], st = editData.style, out = editOut;
  const w = Math.min(width, 440);
  const circle = (p, r) =&gt; d3.range(41).map(i =&gt; ({x: p[0] + r * Math.cos(i * Math.PI / 20),
                                                   y: p[1] + r * Math.sin(i * Math.PI / 20)}));
  const marks = [];
  s.pos.forEach((p, k) =&gt; {                         // one mark per object, so each keeps its own colour
    marks.push(Plot.line(circle(p, editData.reach), {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: st.muted, strokeDasharray: "3,3"}));
    marks.push(Plot.dot([{x: p[0], y: p[1]}], {x: "x", y: "y", r: 9, fill: editData.colours[s.cols[k]],
                                               stroke: st.ink, strokeWidth: 0.6}));
  });
  for (const [m, c] of [["ecot", st.orange], ["dropout", st.blue]]) {
    marks.push(Plot.dot([{x: out[m].slot[0], y: out[m].slot[1]}], {x: "x", y: "y", r: 7, symbol: "diamond",
                                                                    stroke: c, strokeWidth: 2, fill: "none"}));
    marks.push(Plot.arrow([{x1: s.grip[0], y1: s.grip[1], x2: out[m].end[0], y2: out[m].end[1]}],
                          {x1: "x1", y1: "y1", x2: "x2", y2: "y2", stroke: c, strokeWidth: 2.2}));
  }
  marks.push(Plot.dot([{x: s.grip[0], y: s.grip[1]}], {x: "x", y: "y", symbol: "square", r: 6, fill: st.ink}));
  const plot = Plot.plot({
    width: w, height: w, style: {background: st.surface}, marginLeft: 36,
    x: {domain: [-1.5, 1.5], label: null}, y: {domain: [-1.5, 1.5], label: null}, marks
  });
  return html`&lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:4px"&gt;Instruction: &lt;b&gt;reach the ${editData.names[s.cols[s.tgt]]} object&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;${plot}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}








Figure 9: One held-out scene: two coloured objects, the gripper (black square) and the instruction above. Hollow diamonds: what is in each network’s reasoning slot. Arrows: where each network moves the gripper, ECoT in orange and reasoning dropout in blue. The dashed circle around an object is the success radius.




What to try. With its own reasoning, both arrows end near the instructed object in most scenes. Switch the slot to the other object: the orange ECoT arrow swings over to it, while the blue dropout arrow hardly moves. Then choose a point of your own and drag it around: ECoT chases it, as the robot in the lecture followed a person’s corrected reasoning; the dropout network keeps going where the instruction says. Its reasoning is accurate, but it is not what drives its action.


Search through the gap
Eight problems from each difficulty bin, ordered by how often one small-planner sample gets through. Pick a planner, a number of samples and a verifier; your browser draws the samples (with a fixed seed per setting), and the line below gives the exact chance that best-of-N with a perfect verifier succeeds (Equation 3).

searchData = FileAttachment("data/w10-search.json").json()
viewof sBin = Inputs.radio(["easy", "medium", "hard"], {value: "hard", label: "difficulty"})
viewof sIdx = Inputs.range([1, 8], {step: 1, value: 4, label: "problem"})
viewof sPlanner = Inputs.radio(["small", "big"], {value: "small", label: "planner"})
viewof sN = Inputs.select([1, 4, 16, 64], {value: 16, label: "samples N"})
viewof sVer = Inputs.radio(["perfect", "learned"], {value: "perfect", label: "verifier"})












































{
  const probs = searchData.problems.filter(p =&gt; p.bin === sBin), pr = probs[sIdx - 1];
  const cfg = searchData.planners[sPlanner], st = searchData.style, half = searchData.width / 2;
  const seed = 1000 * sIdx + 17 * sN + (sPlanner === "big" ? 7 : 0) + {easy: 0, medium: 100, hard: 200}[sBin];
  const normal = d3.randomNormal.source(d3.randomLcg(seed / 10007));
  const draw = normal(0, 1);
  const cand = d3.range(sN).map(() =&gt; pr.gap + cfg.tau * pr.z + cfg.sigma * draw());
  const ok = cand.map(y =&gt; Math.abs(y - pr.gap) &lt; half);
  const belief = pr.gap + searchData.sigma_v * normal(0, 1)();
  const pick = sVer === "perfect" ? Math.max(0, ok.indexOf(true))
                                  : d3.minIndex(cand, y =&gt; Math.abs(y - belief));
  const path = (y, i) =&gt; [{i, x: -1, y: 0}, {i, x: 0, y}, {i, x: 1, y: 0}];
  const through = cand.flatMap((y, i) =&gt; ok[i] ? path(y, i) : []);
  const blocked = cand.flatMap((y, i) =&gt; ok[i] ? [] : path(y, i));
  const wall = [{x: 0, y1: -1.6, y2: pr.gap - half}, {x: 0, y1: pr.gap + half, y2: 1.6}];
  const w = Math.min(width, 520);
  return Plot.plot({
    width: w, height: Math.round(w * 0.75), style: {background: st.surface}, marginLeft: 36,
    x: {domain: [-1.1, 1.1], label: null}, y: {domain: [-1.6, 1.6], label: null},
    marks: [
      Plot.ruleX(wall, {x: "x", y1: "y1", y2: "y2", stroke: st.muted, strokeWidth: 6}),
      Plot.line(through, {x: "x", y: "y", z: "i", stroke: st.green, strokeOpacity: 0.55, strokeWidth: 1}),
      Plot.line(blocked, {x: "x", y: "y", z: "i", stroke: st.orange, strokeOpacity: 0.55, strokeWidth: 1}),
      Plot.line(path(cand[pick], pick), {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: ok[pick] ? st.green : st.orange, strokeWidth: 3.5}),
      ...(sVer === "learned" ? [Plot.dot([{x: 0, y: belief}], {x: "x", y: "y", r: 6, stroke: st.ink,
                                                                strokeDasharray: "2,2"})] : []),
      Plot.dot([{x: -1, y: 0}, {x: 1, y: 0}], {x: "x", y: "y", r: 5, fill: st.ink})
    ]
  });
}








Figure 10: One problem from the second toy. The wall (grey) has a gap; every sampled path runs from the start (left) through its crossing point on the wall to the goal (right). Green: gets through; orange: hits the wall. The thick path is the verifier’s pick; with the learned verifier, the dotted mark shows where it believes the gap is.





{
  const pr = searchData.problems.filter(p =&gt; p.bin === sBin)[sIdx - 1], p = pr.p[sPlanner];
  const cost = searchData.planners[sPlanner].cost;
  return md`One sample gets through with chance **${(100 * p).toFixed(2)}%**; best-of-${sN} with a perfect verifier succeeds with chance **${(100 * (1 - (1 - p) ** sN)).toFixed(1)}%** · compute: ${sN * cost} small-planner samples`;
}






What to try. Start on a hard problem with the small planner and 16 samples: the paths cluster on one side of the gap, because the planner is wrong about where the gap is, and more samples only fill in the same wrong region. Switch to the big planner with 4 samples, the same compute: its paths aim closer to the gap. Then go to an easy problem, where the small planner’s 16 tries almost always include a good one. Finally choose the learned verifier on an easy problem with 64 samples: whenever its belief (the dotted mark) falls outside the gap, it confidently picks a path that hits the wall.

Interactive

Dream versus reality, by data size








Figure 8: Planning runs on the puck table for world models fitted to the chosen number of random transitions (three models each). Dashed: the runs as imagined, never looking; solid orange: those pushes on the real table; solid green: runs that replan from the real puck.




What to try. At 1,000 transitions the dashed dreams all end on the goal, the orange replays drift off, and the green runs that look end on the goal. Move to 250: the dreams still look perfect (a model always believes itself), but the replays end far away, and even looking does not always help. At 4,000 the replays come closer but still miss.


Asking for more than the motor can give
One 25-step plan from the start, made in the 1,000-transition model (model 0) with pushes limited to the chosen strength. The motor, and the data, stop at 1.

exData = FileAttachment("data/w08-exploit.json").json()
viewof aMax = Inputs.range([1, 3], {step: 0.5, value: 1, label: "strongest push allowed"})
















{
  const r = exData.rows.find(x =&gt; x.a_max === aMax), s = exData.style, w = Math.min(width, 640);
  const pts = (arr, k) =&gt; arr.map((p, i) =&gt; ({x: p[0], y: p[1], i, k}));
  const circle = d3.range(0, 2 * Math.PI + 0.01, 0.1).map(t =&gt; ({x: exData.hill.at[0] + 2 * exData.hill.w * Math.cos(t),
                                                                   y: exData.hill.at[1] + 2 * exData.hill.w * Math.sin(t)}));
  const top = Plot.plot({
    width: w, height: Math.round(w * 0.55), style: {background: s.surface},
    x: {domain: [-2.2, 2.4], label: "x"}, y: {domain: [-1.4, 1.4], label: "y"},
    marks: [
      Plot.areaY(circle, {x: "x", y: "y", fill: s.muted, fillOpacity: 0.3, curve: "linear-closed"}),
      Plot.line(pts(r.imag, "imagined"), {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: s.blue, strokeWidth: 2.5, strokeDasharray: "6,4"}),
      Plot.line(pts(r.real, "real"), {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: s.orange, strokeWidth: 2.5}),
      Plot.dot([{x: exData.goal[0], y: exData.goal[1]}], {x: "x", y: "y", symbol: "times", r: 7, stroke: s.ink, strokeWidth: 2.5})
    ]
  });
  const bottom = Plot.plot({
    width: w, height: 140, marginLeft: 44, style: {background: s.surface},
    x: {label: "plan step"}, y: {label: "push strength", domain: [0, 3.1]},
    marks: [
      Plot.rectY(r.push.map((p, i) =&gt; ({i, p})), {x1: d =&gt; d.i + 0.6, x2: d =&gt; d.i + 1.4, y: "p", fill: s.blue}),
      Plot.ruleY([1], {stroke: s.ink, strokeDasharray: "4,3"})
    ]
  });
  return html`&lt;div&gt;${top}${bottom}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}








Figure 9: The plan in imagination (dashed blue) and the same pushes on the real table (orange), with the hill (grey circle) and the goal (cross). Below: the strength of each push in the plan, against the motor’s limit (dashed). The text compares the plan’s cost in imagination and in reality.





{
  const r = exData.rows.find(x =&gt; x.a_max === aMax);
  return md`Cost in imagination: **${r.c_im.toFixed(1)}** · cost of the same pushes on the real table: **${r.c_re.toFixed(1)}**`;
}






What to try. At 1 the plan respects the motor, and imagination and reality agree closely. Raise the limit: the planner immediately uses the extra strength, the imagined path gets further in 25 steps and its cost drops, but the real puck, held back by the motor, falls behind, and the real cost barely moves. The gap between the two numbers is how much the planner is fooling itself.

