Value iteration, sweep by sweep
Pick a slip probability and step through the sweeps. Cell colour is the current value estimate (blue positive, orange negative); arrows show the greedy action under the current values.

vi = FileAttachment("data/w04-vi.json").json()
viewof slipKey = Inputs.radio(vi.slips, {value: "0.2", label: "slip probability"})
viewof sweep = Inputs.range([0, vi.runs[slipKey].V.length - 1], {step: 1, value: 0, label: "sweep"})























{
  const run = vi.runs[slipKey], k = Math.min(sweep, run.V.length - 1);
  const V = run.V[k], pol = run.pol[k], arrows = ["↑", "→", "↓", "←"];
  const nR = vi.layout.length, nC = vi.layout[0].length;
  const cs = Math.min(64, Math.floor((Math.min(width, 640) - 20) / nC));
  const color = d3.scaleLinear().domain([-1, 0, 1]).range([vi.style.orange, "#ffffff", vi.style.blue]);
  const cells = [];
  vi.layout.forEach((row, r) =&gt; [...row].forEach((ch, c) =&gt; cells.push({r, c, ch})));
  const idx = new Map(vi.cells.map(([r, c], i) =&gt; [r * 100 + c, i]));
  return html`&lt;svg width="${nC * cs + 2}" height="${nR * cs + 2}" style="background:${vi.style.surface}"&gt;
    ${cells.map(({r, c, ch}) =&gt; {
      const i = idx.get(r * 100 + c), x = c * cs + 1, y = r * cs + 1;
      if (ch === "#") return svg`&lt;rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${cs}" height="${cs}" fill="${vi.style.ink}"/&gt;`;
      const term = i === vi.goal || vi.hazards.includes(i);
      const fill = vi.hazards.includes(i) ? vi.style.orange : color(V[i]);
      const label = i === vi.goal ? "G" : vi.hazards.includes(i) ? "X" : arrows[pol[i]];
      return svg`&lt;g&gt;&lt;rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${cs}" height="${cs}" fill="${fill}" stroke="${vi.style.muted}"/&gt;
        &lt;text x="${x + cs / 2}" y="${y + cs * 0.45}" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="middle" font-size="${cs * 0.34}" fill="${vi.style.ink}"&gt;${label}&lt;/text&gt;
        ${term ? "" : svg`&lt;text x="${x + cs / 2}" y="${y + cs * 0.82}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="${cs * 0.2}" fill="${vi.style.ink}"&gt;${V[i].toFixed(2)}&lt;/text&gt;`}
        ${i === vi.start ? svg`&lt;text x="${x + 4}" y="${y + 12}" font-size="11" fill="${vi.style.ink}"&gt;S&lt;/text&gt;` : ""}&lt;/g&gt;`;
    })}&lt;/svg&gt;`;
}








Figure 9: Value iteration on the warehouse grid: the value estimate of every cell after the chosen number of sweeps, and the greedy action it implies. G is the goal (+1), X a hazard (−1), S the start; black cells are walls.





md`Sweep ${Math.min(sweep, vi.runs[slipKey].V.length - 1)} of ${vi.runs[slipKey].V.length - 1} · value of the start: **${vi.runs[slipKey].V[Math.min(sweep, vi.runs[slipKey].V.length - 1)][vi.start].toFixed(3)}**`






What to try. With slip probability 0, step from sweep 0: the goal’s neighbour lights up at sweep 1, and the blue spreads one cell per sweep until it reaches the start at sweep 6; the arrows then run straight along the middle row, past the hazards. Switch to 0.2 and do the same. The values are smaller, because slips waste steps, and the cells in the middle row next to the hazards end up worth less than the cells on the top row. The arrows from the start now point up and round. The last sweeps barely change the colours: they are the long geometric tail of Figure 4.


Q-learning and SARSA: how much exploration?








Figure 10: The greedy routes learned by Q-learning (orange) and SARSA (blue) on the cliff walk after 500 episodes, for the chosen exploration rate ε (the most common route across 20 runs), and the share of runs that learned each length of route.




What to try. Q-learning’s route is the edge at every exploration rate: its values describe the optimal walker, whatever the explorer does. SARSA’s route depends on ε: with ε = 0.01 it is 13 steps, at 0.1 it is 15, and at 0.2 it is 15. The more often its own random moves would push it off the edge, the further it stays from it. Neither is wrong: Q-learning learns the best route for a robot that will stop exploring, SARSA the best route for one that will keep exploring.


Overestimation, live
Choose how many actions there are and how noisy their value estimates are. The browser draws 3,000 sets of estimates (all true values are 0) and shows where the maximum of one set lands, and where the double estimator lands.

viewof nActions = Inputs.range([1, 64], {step: 1, value: 8, label: "actions K"})
viewof noiseSd = Inputs.range([0.1, 2], {step: 0.1, value: 1, label: "noise std"})
















draws = {
  const rand = d3.randomNormal.source(d3.randomLcg(42))(0, noiseSd), out = [];
  for (let t = 0; t &lt; 3000; t++) {
    let best = -Infinity, bi = 0;
    for (let a = 0; a &lt; nActions; a++) { const v = rand(); if (v &gt; best) { best = v; bi = a; } }
    out.push({single: best, double: rand()});     // the second set's value at the chosen action
  }
  return out;
}







{
  const w = Math.min(width, 640), lim = 4 * noiseSd + 0.5;
  const mean = k =&gt; d3.mean(draws, d =&gt; d[k]);
  return Plot.plot({
    width: w, height: 260, marginLeft: 44, style: {background: "#fcfcfb"},
    x: {domain: [-lim, lim], label: "estimate of the best action's value"}, y: {label: "draws", grid: true},
    marks: [
      Plot.rectY(draws, Plot.binX({y: "count"}, {x: "double", fill: "#2a78d6", fillOpacity: 0.45, thresholds: 50})),
      Plot.rectY(draws, Plot.binX({y: "count"}, {x: "single", fill: "#eb6834", fillOpacity: 0.45, thresholds: 50})),
      Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: "#0b0b0b", strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
      Plot.ruleX([mean("single")], {stroke: "#eb6834", strokeWidth: 2}),
      Plot.ruleX([mean("double")], {stroke: "#2a78d6", strokeWidth: 2})
    ]
  });
}








Figure 11: Distribution of the plain maximum (orange) and the double estimator (blue) over 3,000 draws for the chosen number of actions and noise level. The dashed line is the true maximum, 0; the solid lines are the two means.





md`Mean of the plain maximum: **${d3.mean(draws, d =&gt; d.single).toFixed(3)}** · mean of the double estimator: **${d3.mean(draws, d =&gt; d.double).toFixed(3)}**`






What to notice. With one action there is nothing to choose and both estimators agree. Add actions and the orange distribution slides right: the maximum is a winner’s curse, and the more contestants, the luckier the winner. Doubling the noise doubles the bias. The blue distribution stays centred on zero for every setting. The exact orange means for unit noise are in Figure 7.

