Configuration-space explorer
Move the two joint sliders. Left: the arm and the obstacle. Right: the configuration space, where grey cells are the configurations that touch the obstacle and the dot is the arm’s current configuration. Tick the boxes to overlay the straight joint-space line and the planned detour of Figure 3.

cs = FileAttachment("data/w02-cspace.json").json()
viewof q1 = Inputs.range([-180, 180], {step: 2, value: -20, label: "shoulder q1 (deg)"})
viewof q2 = Inputs.range([-180, 180], {step: 2, value: 30, label: "elbow q2 (deg)"})
viewof overlays = Inputs.checkbox(["straight line", "planned detour"], {label: "show"})






























arm = {
  const r = Math.PI / 180, a1 = q1 * r, a12 = (q1 + q2) * r;
  const elbow = [cs.l1 * Math.cos(a1), cs.l1 * Math.sin(a1)];
  const hand = [elbow[0] + cs.l2 * Math.cos(a12), elbow[1] + cs.l2 * Math.sin(a12)];
  // Distance from the obstacle centre to a segment a-b.
  const dist = (a, b) =&gt; {
    const [cx, cy] = cs.obstacle, abx = b[0] - a[0], aby = b[1] - a[1];
    const t = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, ((cx - a[0]) * abx + (cy - a[1]) * aby) / (abx * abx + aby * aby)));
    return Math.hypot(cx - a[0] - t * abx, cy - a[1] - t * aby);
  };
  const hit = dist([0, 0], elbow) &lt; cs.obstacle[2] || dist(elbow, hand) &lt; cs.obstacle[2];
  return {elbow, hand, hit};
}
blockedCells = {
  const cell = 360 / cs.n, out = [];
  cs.blocked.forEach((row, i) =&gt; { for (let j = 0; j &lt; cs.n; j++) if (row[j] === "1")
    out.push({x1: -180 + i * cell, x2: -180 + (i + 1) * cell, y1: -180 + j * cell, y2: -180 + (j + 1) * cell}); });
  return out;
}
















{
  const w = Math.min(width, 640), side = Math.max(220, Math.floor((w - 20) / 2)), wide = 2 * side + 20 &lt;= w;
  const col = arm.hit ? cs.style.orange : cs.style.ink;
  const ws = Plot.plot({
    width: side, height: side, marginLeft: 36, marginBottom: 32, style: {background: cs.style.surface},
    x: {domain: [-1.9, 1.9], label: "x"}, y: {domain: [-1.9, 1.9], label: "y"},
    marks: [
      Plot.line(d3.range(61).map(k =&gt; [cs.obstacle[0] + cs.obstacle[2] * Math.cos(k * Math.PI / 30),
                                        cs.obstacle[1] + cs.obstacle[2] * Math.sin(k * Math.PI / 30)]),
                {fill: cs.style.neutral, stroke: cs.style.muted}),
      Plot.line([[0, 0], arm.elbow, arm.hand], {stroke: col, strokeWidth: 4}),
      Plot.dot([[0, 0], arm.elbow, arm.hand], {fill: col, r: 4})
    ]
  });
  const marks = [Plot.rect(blockedCells, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y1: "y1", y2: "y2", fill: cs.style.muted, fillOpacity: 0.55})];
  if (overlays.includes("straight line")) marks.push(Plot.line(cs.straight, {stroke: cs.style.orange, strokeWidth: 2}));
  if (overlays.includes("planned detour")) marks.push(Plot.dot(cs.path, {fill: cs.style.blue, r: 1.6}));
  marks.push(Plot.dot([[q1, q2]], {fill: col, stroke: "white", r: 6}));
  const cfg = Plot.plot({
    width: side, height: side, marginLeft: 44, marginBottom: 32, style: {background: cs.style.surface},
    x: {domain: [-180, 180], label: "shoulder q1 (deg)", ticks: [-180, -90, 0, 90, 180]},
    y: {domain: [-180, 180], label: "elbow q2 (deg)", ticks: [-180, -90, 0, 90, 180]},
    marks
  });
  return html`&lt;div style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:20px"&gt;${ws}${cfg}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}








Figure 8: The arm in the workspace (left) and its configuration in configuration space (right). The arm and the dot turn orange when the arm touches the obstacle.





md`Hand at (${arm.hand[0].toFixed(2)}, ${arm.hand[1].toFixed(2)}) · **${arm.hit ? "touching the obstacle" : "clear of the obstacle"}**`






What to try. Start at the defaults, the start configuration of Figure 3. Raise the shoulder angle with the elbow fixed at 30°: at a shoulder angle of 20° the forearm reaches the disc and the dot enters the grey region. Now set the shoulder to 40° and move the elbow through its whole range: the arm touches the disc at every elbow angle, because the upper arm alone already passes through it. At a shoulder angle of 70°, only bending the elbow downwards (negative angles) brings the forearm into the disc. Tick both overlays: the straight line cuts through the grey region, the planned detour stays in white cells, and where the detour reaches an edge of the square it continues on the opposite edge, because the angles wrap around.


PID tuner
Set the gains, the control frequency, the waypoint scaling, and whether gravity acts. The simulation is the same as the toy’s: a 1 kg, 0.5 m rod lifted from -30° to 60° in 1 s, then held, with the physics stepped every millisecond and the controller updating at the chosen frequency.

pid = FileAttachment("data/w02-pid.json").json()
viewof Kp = Inputs.range([0, 80], {step: 1, value: 30, label: "Kp"})
viewof Ki = Inputs.range([0, 150], {step: 5, value: 60, label: "Ki"})
viewof Kd = Inputs.range([0, 6], {step: 0.25, value: 2, label: "Kd"})
